WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.000
Film Courage: What is the most amount of time
you’ve put into a screenplay and was it

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.000
worth it?

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.499
David Wappel, Screenwriter: The most amount
of time I’ve put into a screenplay?

00:00:07.499 --> 00:00:13.620
It’s tough because I haven’t measured
exactly how much time I’ve worked on it

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:25.480
but I know that the longest from an idea to
a finished draft was five years and that’s

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.960
just to the end of the screenplay.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.020
That’s just it still sitting there.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.020
No film has been made.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:40.470
That is five years what is to me a finished
draft sort of working on it sporadically sometimes

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:41.470
every day.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:47.650
Other times it kind of sat dormant for a few
months and I worked on other projects or whatever

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:52.670
but that’s the longest of anything I’ve
ever done (five years).

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:57.590
Film Courage: Why did it take so long?

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.010
Maybe that’s not a long time?

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:08.270
David: I think it took awhile and it was written
in sort of fits and starts because it was

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:16.070
one of the first projects I started once I
started seriously writing so I didn’t know

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:21.020
yet…not necessarily that I didn’t know
what I was doing because at times I still

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.020
don’t feel like know what I I’m doing
when I write a screenplay but I didn’t know

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:28.530
a process that worked for me in terms of creating
something.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.650
So I just started writing.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:36.799
I had a loose outline and I just started writing
and I kept hitting dead end after dead end

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:41.720
or I would figure something out that didn’t
feel like a dead end but it wasn’t interesting

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.720
to me.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.049
It’s just wasn’t…nothing was happening.

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:54.610
So I essentially had to continuously start
it over and over because I didn’t have a

00:01:54.610 --> 00:02:04.410
process yet that I felt conformable with and
I didn’t really know what I was interested

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.410
in writing about.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:10.119
I had a story idea but I didn’t know why
that was a story idea that interested me.

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:14.739
Moving forward it was always important to
me that when I was writing something that

00:02:14.739 --> 00:02:22.559
I always understood that before I really got
into it why I am interested in that story

00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:26.599
and why I am going to take that across the
finish line.

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:31.790
That has been a really useful tool to help
with other story ideas that I’ve had that

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:32.879
I think would be g great movies.

00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:38.591
I think they are so cool but I am maybe not
the one to write that so I shouldn’t go

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:39.790
after that yet.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:44.650
But there is still something that might interest
me so I’ll keep it in there and at some

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.709
point I go Oh, that’s what I’m interested
in.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:53.299
That’s the theme that comes out of that
story that resonates with me and now I am

00:02:53.299 --> 00:02:54.689
ready to start digging into that.

00:02:54.689 --> 00:03:01.200
Film Courage: It sounds like that story has
a lot of meaning for you (the initial idea)?

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.829
David: Now it does.

00:03:03.829 --> 00:03:08.090
It didn’t at first because I didn’t have
anything but once I discovered that I was

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:15.230
able to finish it and bring it across the
finish line and understand that I was interested

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.189
in writing about.

00:03:17.189 --> 00:03:24.180
Film Courage: Do you think you will have this
made at some point or is it almost like…you

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:30.989
know how some people say this isn’t the
story for me to make but it’s a great idea?

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:36.849
David: I think at this point it’s a story
that I would love to see made but in terms

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:43.049
of where I am in my career right now it’s
a very big project.

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:49.620
This was at a time when I was writing before
considerations of my career.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:56.680
What is the best story that I can write and
get into the hands of producers I know and

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.219
have relationships with.

00:03:58.219 --> 00:04:03.480
So this project just isn’t a good fit for
the people I know right now so it’s still

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.609
just kind of sitting dormant.

00:04:04.609 --> 00:04:11.290
It’s been out to a few people to read for
feedback and thoughts but nothing has really

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:12.290
happened with it yet.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:13.290
Question For The Viewers: What is the most
amount of time you have put into writing a

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:13.296
screenplay and was it worth it?

